## ⚠️ Section 2: CRITICAL - Read Standards BEFORE Coding

**Optimized Pattern:** Mandate reading standards documents BEFORE any code is written. This is the #1 quality multiplier.

```markdown
## ⚠️ CRITICAL: Read Standards BEFORE Coding

**BEFORE implementing ANY feature, you MUST read these standards documents:**

### 📖 Required Reading (in `/docs/`)

1. **`docs/coding-standards.md`** ⭐ MANDATORY
   - Language-specific conventions ([Python/TypeScript/etc])
   - File naming patterns (snake_case, kebab-case, PascalCase)
   - Function/class/variable naming rules
   - Import ordering, error handling, logging patterns
   - **Read this FIRST before writing any code**

2. **`docs/component-patterns.md`** (For [React/Vue/etc] components)
   - Component composition patterns
   - Props conventions and destructuring
   - Custom hooks patterns
   - Performance optimization (React.memo, useMemo, useCallback)
   - Error boundaries and error handling
   - **Read before building any [framework] component**

3. **`docs/state-management-patterns.md`** (For [Redux/Pinia/etc])
   - [State library] for API integration (server state)
   - [State library] for global client state
   - Selectors and memoization
   - Async patterns for complex flows
   - When to use global vs local state
   - **Read before implementing state management**

4. **`docs/styling-patterns.md`** (For [Tailwind/CSS-in-JS/etc])
   - Component styling patterns and variants
   - Theme and design tokens (colors, spacing, typography)
   - Responsive design (mobile-first)
   - Accessibility (focus states, ARIA, semantic HTML)
   - Utility composition and best practices
   - **Read before styling any component**

5. **`docs/api-patterns.md`** (For API development)
   - [Multi-tenant authorization patterns (CRITICAL for security)]
   - REST API design patterns (CRUD, pagination, error handling)
   - Request/response formats
   - [Webhook patterns if applicable]
   - **Read before implementing any API endpoint**

6. **`docs/database-patterns.md`** (For database work)
   - [Row-Level Security (RLS) patterns (CRITICAL for multi-tenancy)]
   - Complete schema definitions [with RLS policies]
   - Query patterns ([ORM] & [language])
   - Migration patterns
   - **Read before writing any database query**

7. **`docs/testing-patterns.md`** (For all code)
   - Test pyramid (unit, integration, E2E)
   - Testing patterns for [languages/frameworks]
   - [RLS policy testing (multi-tenant isolation)]
   - Coverage requirements ([X]%+ overall)
   - **Read before writing tests**

8. **`docs/error-handling-patterns.md`** (For all code)
   - Error handling in API endpoints (try/catch, error middleware)
   - Frontend error boundaries and user-facing messages
   - Retry logic and exponential backoff
   - Error logging with correlation IDs
   - **Read before implementing any error handling**

9. **`docs/performance-patterns.md`** (For optimization)
   - Database query optimization (N+1 prevention, indexes)
   - Caching strategies (Redis, CDN, browser cache)
   - Frontend performance (React.memo, useMemo, useCallback)
   - Pagination and lazy loading
   - **Read before optimizing performance-critical code**

10. **`docs/async-patterns.md`** (For async operations)
    - Promise patterns (async/await, error handling)
    - Parallel vs sequential execution
    - Race conditions and prevention
    - Debouncing/throttling user input
    - **Read before writing async code**

11. **`docs/logging-patterns.md`** (For observability)
    - Structured logging format (JSON with context)
    - Log levels (DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR)
    - What to log (and what NOT to log - PII)
    - Correlation IDs for request tracing
    - **Read before adding logging statements**

### 🚨 Why This Matters

- **Consistency:** Following standards ensures all code follows the same patterns
- **Security:** [Multi-tenant isolation/Security] patterns prevent data leaks
- **Quality:** Testing patterns ensure comprehensive coverage
- **Efficiency:** Don't reinvent the wheel - patterns are already established

### ✅ Development Workflow

**For EVERY feature:**
1. ✅ **Check user story status** in `docs/high-level-user-stories.md` to see if it's complete, in progress, or planned
2. ✅ **Read detailed spec** in `/docs/features/us-XXX-*.md` for acceptance criteria and implementation notes
3. ✅ Read relevant standards documents in `/docs/`
4. ✅ Review existing similar code for established patterns
5. ✅ Implement following the standards
6. ✅ Write tests per testing-patterns.md
7. ✅ Verify quality gates pass
8. ✅ **Update** `docs/high-level-user-stories.md` status when complete

**If you deviate from standards:**
- Document WHY in code comments
- Update the standard if the new pattern is better
- Get user approval for major changes
```

**Quick Guide:**
- **Create `docs/high-level-user-stories.md`** as a central tracker for all user stories
  - Include quick reference table with status, phase, backend/frontend completion
  - Link to detailed specs in `/docs/features/us-XXX-*.md`
  - Update status after completing each story (make this part of workflow)
- List all standards docs that MUST exist in `/docs/` (aim for 11+ pattern documents)
- Use visual markers (⚠️, ⭐, ✅) for scanning
- Make reading standards step 0 of every feature
- Include domain-specific standards (security, multi-tenancy, etc.)
- Include runtime pattern docs (error-handling, logging, performance, async) for code quality
- Mandate checking user story tracker BEFORE starting any feature

